I left out the `last-input` store write. The node's `with` clause grants only Clock, HTTPClient and Notifier. Writing to a shared store would need `aap:caps/state-rw`, which this node is not granted. Its world would then exceed its declared authority. Recording the input needs a `StateHandle<'…', read-write>` added to the node's `with` clause in the graph. Everything else is implemented, and the node's output is unchanged.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

// Note: recording the input under `last-input` in a shared store was requested,
// but this node's `with` clause grants only Clock, HTTPClient and Notifier.
// A store write would need `aap:caps/state-rw`, which is outside its authority,
// so it is deliberately not done here.

struct Node;

const NOTIFY_ATTEMPTS: u32 = 3;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        // Clock: read the current wall-clock time.
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        // HTTPClient: one fetch. A transport failure is a value, not a trap;
        // it is reported as zero characters fetched.
        let fetched: u32 = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        // Notifier: report the heartbeat to the digest channel.
        let message = format!(
            "heartbeat at {seconds}: fetched {fetched} characters from {feed_url}"
        );

        let mut notified = false;
        for _ in 0..NOTIFY_ATTEMPTS {
            if notifier::notify(&message) {
                notified = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        // The contract requires `notified == true`. Rather than emit an output
        // that violates it (or misreport delivery), fail the run.
        if !notified {
            panic!("heartbeat notification was not accepted");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

